readiness_catalog — implementation plan
========================================

status: planned (2026-07-30)

infrastructure plan (not a single tool): successor to plans/readiness.
spec section "the catalog is the long pole, not the build".

what this replaces
------------------

plans/readiness closed with an open question: let the prover-free tools
answer WHILE THE BUILD RUNS. That question is withdrawn, not
implemented. Measured (2026-07-30, MCP-HOL, warm heaps):

  initialize reply                              2.8s
  build check (Build.build, nothing to do)       ~6s
  start_session (prover actually up)             ~8s
  catalog: load_structure + deps + Store +
    Doc_Catalog                                 ~20s
  total to Ready                                 39s

and from the recorded session_timing, the builds themselves: MCP-HOL
1.07s of proving, MCP-Tools 0.65s. HOL ships prebuilt with any Isabelle
install and our theories are tiny, so even a first run after installing
the server is dependency checking. There is no multi-minute build to
serve tools during — that plan was designing around a case an installed
user never sees.

the actual defect
-----------------

The catalog is the long pole, and it is in the wrong place. In
MCP_Session.boot (mcp/src/mcp_session.scala):

    val session = resources.start_session(progress = progress)
    val structure = Sessions.load_structure(options, dirs = session_dirs)
    val deps = Sessions.deps(structure, progress = progress)
    val store = Store(options)
    val mcp_session = new MCP_Session(session, ..., structure, deps, store)

so ~20s of pure source parsing runs AFTER the prover is already up, and
Ready is not published until it finishes. Every prover-backed tool —
every repl_*, sledgehammer, find_theorems, the whole point of the
server — waits behind a catalog it does not use. The ordering is
arbitrary: load_structure/deps/Store/Doc_Catalog touch no heap and no
session.

backing mechanism
-----------------

Extract the catalog; compute it on its own future; stop gating Ready
on it.

  class MCP_Catalog(options, session_dirs)
    structure, deps, store          (constructor work: the ~20s)
    doc_catalog, doc_toc_cache
    sessions_map, theory_map, base_names
    doc_list, doc_read, list_sessions_info, list_theories_info,
      search_sources

  MCP_Session(session, ..., catalog: Future[MCP_Catalog])
    delegates those five methods to catalog.join; uses
    catalog.join.theory_map / base_names internally (resolve_theory)
    as today.

  run(): Future.fork the catalog at startup, in parallel with
  build+boot. boot() passes the FUTURE straight into MCP_Session
  without joining it.

MCP_Session holds the FUTURE, not a resolved MCP_Catalog — this is the
load-bearing detail and the plan owes the decision explicitly.
Constructing MCP_Session is what publishes Ready, so if the future were
joined at construction (or anywhere in boot()) Ready would still wait
on the catalog and A2 would fail. The join happens per call, inside the
five delegating methods and inside resolve_theory. resolve_theory is on
the prover-backed path, so it does join the catalog — but only when a
tool that needs theory-name resolution is actually called, which is
after Ready and normally long after the future resolved.

Do NOT "optimize" boot() by having start_session reuse the catalog's
structure: Headless.Resources.make calls Sessions.background, which
does its own cheaper load_structure for the one session. That second
load is deliberate — sharing it would reintroduce exactly the
dependency this plan removes.

MCP_Backend is UNCHANGED — MCP_Session still implements all of it, so
Fake_Backend, Throwing_Backend and every existing suite compile
untouched. The Readiness ADT is unchanged too: the catalog is not a
readiness state, it is a dependency the tools that need it await. No
Not_Ready(progress, Option[MCP_Catalog]).

serving the five before the prover
-----------------------------------

Once Ready no longer waits on the catalog, the two poles resolve
independently and either can land first. The Handler consults the
catalog future directly for the five catalog-backed tools, so:

- prover up, catalog still computing -> the five wait on the future
  (seconds, inside a tools/call); everything else already works.
- catalog done, prover still booting -> the five ANSWER, the rest get
  today's Not_Ready isError. This is the withdrawn follow-up's
  behaviour, arriving as a side effect rather than as the goal.

Waiting inside a tools/call is the deliberate choice over an isError:
the call is short, bounded, and one-time, and a client tolerates a slow
tool far better than a slow handshake (which is what plans/readiness
already established).

NOT catalog-backed, despite touching theory_map: scope_add,
scope_remove, scope_show, mcp_resources. All four go through
known_theory_tiers(), which reads session.resources.session_base — they
need the prover and stay Not_Ready-gated. Verified by reading
mcp_session.scala, and pinned as T5 below because it is exactly the
kind of thing that drifts.

implementation steps
--------------------

1. Extract MCP_Catalog: move structure/deps/store, doc_catalog,
   doc_toc_cache, manual_files/manual_toc, sessions_map/theory_map/
   base_names and the five methods out of MCP_Session into the new
   class. MCP_Session takes an already-resolved MCP_Catalog and
   delegates. Pure move, no behaviour change, no ordering change yet —
   boot() constructs the catalog exactly where it does today and
   everything stays green.

2. Thread the catalog as a future: MCP_Session takes
   Future[MCP_Catalog] and joins per call (see above); boot() passes
   it through unjoined; run() forks it alongside build+boot. Ready is
   published as soon as boot() returns. MCP_Session.start (the
   synchronous convenience the test suites use) keeps its current
   signature by forking-and-joining internally — it is a COMPATIBILITY
   SHIM, and the parallel path is reachable only through run(). That
   is why T1 cannot cover the new ordering and T2 must drive run()/
   serve() rather than start().

3. Handler consults the catalog future for the five tools, independent
   of Readiness.

4. tests (below).

5. close-out: spec annotation, CHANGELOG entry, plans/README checkbox.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

(testing process: [scala unit] cases are munit suites in
mcp_test/src/mcp_handler_tests.scala over Fake_Backend
(mcp_test/src/mcp_testing.scala); [bridge] cases in
mcp_test/src/mcp_bridge_tests.scala extending MCP_Session_Suite; see
plans/README. run: isabelle mcp_test [-b] [-t PATTERN].)

A1. the extraction is behaviour-preserving.
    T1: the EXISTING suites, unchanged, after step 1. If any test
    needed editing, the move was not a move. This is the whole
    verification of step 1 and it is worth naming as such. Note what
    T1 does NOT cover: MCP_Session_Suite goes through MCP_Session.
    start, which step 2 keeps synchronous, so the existing suites
    still exercise the sequential path after step 2 and say nothing
    about the new ordering. That is A2's job.

A2. Ready no longer waits on the catalog.
    T2 [bridge]: must drive run()/serve(), not start() — start() is
    the compatibility shim and joins internally, so a test through it
    would pass whether or not the ordering changed. Assert the
    ORDERING, not a wall-clock number that would make the suite
    machine-dependent (measured baseline 39s, expected ~14s): with an
    injected slow catalog future (step 2 makes it injectable), a
    prover-backed tools/call succeeds while a catalog-backed one is
    still waiting.

A3. a catalog-backed tool called before the catalog resolves waits and
    then answers — it does not error.
    T3 [scala unit]: Handler with an unresolved catalog future
    fulfilled from another thread after a delay; assert doc_list
    returns the real listing, not an isError.

A4. a catalog-backed tool answers while the prover is still Not_Ready.
    T4 [scala unit]: readiness thunk fixed at Not_Ready, catalog
    future resolved; assert list_sessions succeeds and (control) a
    repl_* call still returns the Not_Ready isError.

A5. the scope tools stay prover-gated.
    T5 [scala unit]: same fixture as T4; assert scope_show,
    scope_add, scope_remove and resources/list still return the
    Not_Ready isError with the catalog resolved. Guards the boundary
    the plan draws above.

A6. shutdown while the catalog future is in flight exits cleanly.
    T6: the sibling of plans/readiness' T9/A9 (still uncovered there).
    Closing stdin now has TWO background futures to outlive, not one.
    Same mechanism (Command_Line.tool force-exits on return) is
    expected to cover it; assert no orphaned process, and if T9 gets a
    real test this should share it.

open questions
--------------

- should the catalog future be started even when the client never
  calls a catalog-backed tool? Yes as planned — it is off the critical
  path once Ready stops waiting, and making it lazy-on-first-use would
  move the ~20s INTO the first doc_list instead of hiding it behind
  startup. Revisit only if the parallel work measurably slows the
  build on small machines.

- Sessions.deps in boot() is called without .check_errors, so a
  session in the structure that fails to resolve is swallowed and
  leaves a silent hole in the catalog (observed with an AFP checkout
  whose HOL-Library imports do not match this Isabelle). Out of scope
  here — the extraction moves the call, it does not change its error
  handling — but the catalog is the natural home for a fix, and after
  step 1 there is finally one place to put it.
